content='<think>\nOkay, so I need to generate an Ansible Playbook based on the given tasks. Let me go through each task step by step to make sure I understand what\'s required.\n\nFirst, the playbook must start with \'---\' on the very first line, so I\'ll make sure to include that. It also needs to be a valid YAML file, adhering to yamllint and ansible-lint standards. That means proper indentation, correct syntax, and no trailing spaces.\n\nThe tasks are to be included in an Ansible Role under the tasks/ directory, specifically tasks/main.yml. So, I\'ll structure the playbook accordingly, without any playbook-level headers like hosts or vars.\n\nLet\'s look at each task:\n\nTask 1 is to import assert.yml. In Ansible, importing files is done using the import_tasks module. So, I\'ll create a task with name "Import assertion tasks", use the import_tasks module, and specify the file as assert.yml. The state should be present.\n\nTask 2 is about managing automatic service management for apt-based systems. The module for this is apt_autostart. I\'ll set the name to "Manage apt-based services autostart", the state to the variable apt_autostart_state, and the service to \'*\' to affect all services.\n\nTask 3 is to disable automatic service management on installation. This task runs only if apt_autostart_state is set to "disabled". I\'ll use the file module to create /usr/sbin/policy-rc.d with content "exit 101" and mode 755. I\'ll register the output to a variable for logging purposes.\n\nTask 4 is the opposite of Task 3—it enables automatic service management if apt_autostart_state is "enabled". Here, I\'ll use file again with state=absent to remove the policy-rc.d file and ensure it\'s gone, using register to capture the output.\n\nI need to ensure that each task has the correct name, module, and parameters. Also, the order is important: Task 1 comes first, then 2, followed by 3 and 4, as they depend on the state.\n\nI should also make sure that the variables are correctly referenced and that the tasks are conditional based on the variable values. Using \'when\' conditions might not be necessary if the module parameters handle the states correctly, but I\'ll double-check that.\n\nFinally, I\'ll structure the YAML correctly, ensuring each task is a list under the main playbook block, and that the entire playbook is enclosed within the \'---\' markers.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Import assertion tasks\n  import_tasks: assert.yml\n\n- name: Manage apt-based services autostart\n  apt_autostart:\n    name: "*"\n    state: "{{ apt_autostart_state }}"\n\n- name: Disable automatic service management on installation\n  file:\n    path: /usr/sbin/policy-rc.d\n    content: "exit 101"\n    mode: \'755\'\n    state: present\n  when: apt_autostart_state == "disabled"\n  register: disable_policy_rc_d\n\n- name: Enable automatic service management on installation\n  file:\n    path: /usr/sbin/policy-rc.d\n    state: absent\n  when: apt_autostart_state == "enabled"\n  register: enable_policy_rc_d\n```' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10-02T09:44:45.910192803Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 10449588618, 'load_duration': 105038356, 'prompt_eval_count': 443, 'prompt_eval_duration': 93877968, 'eval_count': 699, 'eval_duration': 10249086442, 'model_name': 'deepseek-r1:14b'} id='run--3de740d1-077a-47c2-b80d-745e54c45280-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 443, 'output_tokens': 699, 'total_tokens': 1142}
