r example, for RedHat, the condition would be ansible_distribution == \'RedHat\' and ansible_package_state == \'package\'.\n\nWait, but in Ansible, the distribution fact is different. For RedHat, it might be \'RedHat\', and for others, like \'Fedora\' or \'Ubuntu\', it\'s their respective names. So, the conditions should correctly target each OS.\n\nAlso, the setup files (like setup-RedHat.yml) should be in a specific directory. Since this is a role, the files would be in tasks/, so I don\'t need to specify a path, just the filename.\n\nPutting it all together, the playbook will have six tasks. The first task sets the fact, and the next five include the respective setup files with their conditions.\n\nI should also ensure that the YAML is correctly formatted, with proper indentation and no trailing spaces. Each task is a separate entry under the main.yml file.\n\nLet me double-check the conditions. For each OS, the include_tasks is only when the package state is correct and the distribution matches. For pip installation, it\'s the opposite of the package state.\n\nI think that\'s all. Now, I\'ll structure the YAML accordingly.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Set ansible_package_state fact\n  set_fact:\n    ansible_package_state: "package"\n  when: ansible_distribution is defined and ansible_distribution == \'RedHat\' or ansible_distribution == \'Fedora\' or ansible_distribution == \'Ubuntu\' or ansible_distribution == \'Debian\'\n\n- name: Set up Ansible on RedHat\n  include_tasks: setup-RedHat.yml\n  when:\n    - ansible_package_state == \'package\'\n    - ansible_distribution == \'RedHat\'\n\n- name: Set up Ansible on Fedora\n  include_tasks: setup-Fedora.yml\n  when:\n    - ansible_package_state == \'package\'\n    - ansible_distribution == \'Fedora\'\n\n- name: Set up Ansible on Ubuntu\n  include_tasks: setup-Ubuntu.yml\n  when:\n    - ansible_package_state == \'package\'\n    - ansible_distribution == \'Ubuntu\'\n\n- name: Set up Ansible on Debian\n  include